# Ignore rules scoped to the slatwall-ts/ subtree. Git applies every pattern below relative
# to THIS directory, so nothing here reaches the legacy CFML tree.
#
# That scoping is the reason the file exists. The repository-root .gitignore covers the legacy
# application and none of its entries covers node_modules, dist or coverage; closing that gap
# here rather than there keeps the root file, and the whole CFML tree, byte-for-byte unchanged,
# so this port stays a single additive diff.
#
# The list is deliberately short — exactly three patterns and nothing else: every entry
# names a generated directory this subtree actually produces, and speculative boilerplate
# is omitted rather than copied in (AAP 0.7.3 standard 9, guideline 0.8.2/1). The three
# directory names are shared with tsconfig.json "exclude", tsconfig.build.json "outDir"
# and "exclude", eslint.config.mjs "ignores", jest.config.ts "coverageDirectory" and
# "testPathIgnorePatterns", and the output directory in build/esbuild.mjs. All six must
# keep saying the same three words, or a build product ends up tracked.
#
# Nothing else belongs here, and the omissions are decisions rather than oversights. No
# credential file is listed: the plan's contract for this file is exactly the three
# directories above (AAP 0.3.1, 0.4.1.2, 0.5.6.2), configuration reaches the service only
# through the process environment (AAP 0.4.3.5, 0.8.3.9), no file in this subtree holds a
# credential, and the committed .env.example carries variable names with empty values and
# is therefore tracked on purpose. No editor, OS or tool-cache pattern is listed either:
# each would be boilerplate for an artifact this subtree never emits.

# Installed dependency tree, materialised by `npm ci` from package-lock.json. The
# lockfile itself is deliberately NOT ignored: a committed lockfile is what makes the
# exact-version resolution reproducible (AAP 0.7.3 standard 5).
node_modules/

# Compiled and bundled output. Both routes to an artifact emit here — `tsc -p
# tsconfig.build.json` (outDir "dist") and `node build/esbuild.mjs` (bundle, source maps
# and metafile.json) — and build products are never committed.
dist/

# Jest coverage reports (jest.config.ts, coverageDirectory: 'coverage').
coverage/

# THIS FILE IS ALSO PRETTIER'S IGNORE LIST, SO NO .prettierignore EXISTS (F20).
# Prettier's CLI defaults `--ignore-path` to `[.gitignore, .prettierignore]` — its own
# `prettier --help` on 3.9.6 says so verbatim — and it skips whichever of the two is
# absent. So the three directories above are already excluded from
# `npx prettier --check .` without a second list to keep in sync.
#
# A .prettierignore did exist and was DELETED: it appears in no AAP inventory (0.2.1.7
# and 0.4.1.2 declare .prettierrc.json and this file, not it), and removing it was
# verified not to change the result. `npx prettier --check .` passes with dist/ present,
# and package-lock.json — the one tracked file the deleted list also named — is
# byte-identical to Prettier's own JSON output, so checking it is a no-op rather than a
# churn risk. Do not re-add the file to silence a hypothetical: measure first, exactly
# as this note did.

# A real .env is a local developer convenience that may hold live database credentials,
# so it never enters git; configuration reaches the service through the environment and
# no credential is hardcoded (AAP 0.8.3.9). The pattern is the exact filename and not a
# wildcard, so the required, committed .env.example — variable names only, no values —
# is never swallowed.
.env
